  "worked_solution": "We note that $m=0$ cannot be a solution since division by $0$ is undefined. \n\nThe equation has the denominators $2{m}^{2},$ ${m}^{2}$ and $m.$ So first, we find the least common multiple (or $\\text{LCM}$) of the denominators. We find that\n\n$$\n\\text{LCM}(m,{m}^{2},2{m}^{2})=2{m}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we multiply both sides of the equation by $2{m}^{2},$ and simplify: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}} & =\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}} \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m}-\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}) \\\\ 2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{m})-2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{3m+4}{2{m}^{2}}) & =2{m}^{2}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{{m}^{2}}) \\\\ 2m\\cdot \\cancel{m}\\cdot (\\frac{3}{\\cancel{m}})-\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{3m+4}{\\cancel{2{m}^{2}}}) & =2\\cdot \\cancel{{m}^{2}}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{\\cancel{{m}^{2}}}) \\\\ 2m\\cdot 3-(3m+4) & =2\\cdot 1 \\\\ 6m-(3m+4) & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now have a linear equation in $m,$ and we can solve this using the addition and multiplication principles:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6m-(3m+4) & =2 \\\\ 6m-3m-4 & =2 \\\\ 3m & =6 \\\\ m & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we recall that we cannot allow $m=0$ as a solution. But since $m=2\\ne 0,$ we conclude that the solution is $m=2.$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
